Recall that in this and subsequent sections we are working on the assumption that~\eqref{to-contradict} and its conditioned variant~\eqref{to-contradict-avg} hold, aiming to prove that $k = 0$ and hence conclude (the contrapositive of) Proposition~ef{de-prop}. Recall also that $X_1, X_2, 	ilde X_1, 	ilde X_2$ are independent random variables, with $X_1,	ilde X_1$ copies of $X_1$ and $X_2,	ilde X_2$ copies of $X_2$. In this section we establish the upper bound~\eqref{first-est}, which was that \[ I_1 \coloneqq  igI{ X_1+X_2 : 	ilde X_1 + X_2 | X_1+X_2+	ilde X_1+	ilde X_2 } \leqslant 2 \eta k. \] We apply \Cref{cor-fibre} (and Remark ef{rem:char2}) with the choice \[ (Y_1,Y_2,Y_3,Y_4) \coloneqq (X_1, X_2, 	ilde X_2, 	ilde X_1). \] It gives  egin{align} 
onumber &\dist{X_1+	ilde X_2}{X_2+	ilde X_1} +  igdist{X_1|X_1+	ilde X_2}{X_2|X_2+	ilde X_1} \ &\quad +  igI{ X_1+ X_2 : 	ilde X_1 + X_2 \,|\, X_1 + X_2 + 	ilde X_1 + 	ilde X_2 } = 2k, \end{align} since $\dist{X_1}{X_2} = k$. Applying~\eqref{to-contradict},~\eqref{to-contradict-avg}, we have  egin{align*} \dist{X_1+	ilde X_2}{X_2+	ilde X_1} \geqslant k &- \costa{X_1+	ilde X_2} \& \qquad- \costb{X_2+	ilde X_1} \end{align*} and  egin{align} 
onumber & \dist{X_1|X_1+	ilde X_2}{X_2|X_2+	ilde X_1} \ 
onumber & \qquad\quad \geqslant k - \costa{X_1 | X_1 + 	ilde X_2} \ & \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad - \costb{X_2 | X_2 + 	ilde X_1}. \end{align} It therefore suffices to prove that  egin{align} 
onumber &(\dist{X_1^0}{X_1+	ilde X_2}-\dist{X_1^0}{X_1})+ (\dist{X_2^0}{X_2+	ilde X_1}-\dist{X_2^0}{X_2})\ &\qquad \qquad +(\dist{X_1^0}{X_1|X_1+	ilde X_2}-\dist{X_1^0}{X_1}) 
onumber \ & \qquad \qquad \qquad \qquad +(\dist{X_2^0}{X_2|X_2+	ilde X_1}-\dist{X_2^0}{X_2}) \leqslant 2k. \end{align} We pause to state some lemmas which we will use for bounding a number of similar expressions, both here and in the next two sections. The first is a bound relating conditioned and unconditioned variants of the Ruzsa distance.  egin{lemma} Suppose that $(X, Z)$ and $(Y, W)$ are random variables, where $X, Y$ take values in some abelian group. Then \[ \dist{X | Z}{Y | W} \leqslant \dist{X}{Y} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{X : Z} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{Y : W}.\] \end{lemma}  egin{proof} The definition of conditional distance is given in~\eqref{cond-dist-def}. Using the alternative expression~\eqref{cond-dist-alt}, if $(X',Z'), (Y',W')$ are independent copies of the variables $(X,Z)$, $(Y,W)$, we have  egin{align*} \dist{X | Z}{Y | W} &= \ent{X'-Y'|Z',W'} - 	frac{1}{2} \ent{X'|Z'} - 	frac{1}{2}\ent{Y'|W'} \ &\leqslant \ent{X'-Y'}- 	frac{1}{2} \ent{X'|Z'} - 	frac{1}{2}\ent{Y'|W'} \ &= \dist{X'}{Y'} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{X' : Z'} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{Y' : W'}. \end{align*} Here, in the middle step we used~\eqref{cond-dec}, and in the last step we used the definitions of $\dist{-}{-}$ and $\I{-}$. \end{proof} In the proof of the next lemma we will use the fact that, for any independent random variables $X, Y, Z$ taking values in an abelian group, we have the inequality  egin{equation} \ent{X + Y + Z} - \ent{X + Y} \leqslant \ent{Y+Z} - \ent{Y}. \end{equation} This is a result of Madiman~\cite[Theorem I]{madiman}, and is a more general form of an inequality of Kaimanovich and Vershik~\cite[Proposition 1.3]{kv}. It can be viewed as an entropy analogue of an inequality of Pl"unnecke~\cite{plunnecke}. For the convenience of the reader, we give the proof in \Cref{entropy-app}.  egin{lemma} Let $X, Y, Z$ be random variables taking values in some abelian group, and with $Y, Z$ independent. Then we have ootnote{We thank Floris van Doorn for noting a sign discrepancy in a previous version of this statement, which was uncovered as part of the effort to formalize the results of this paper in {	t Lean 4}.}  egin{align}
onumber \dist{X}{Y - Z} - \dist{X}{Y} & \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{Y-Z} - \ent{Y}) \ & = 	frac{1}{2} \dist{Y}{Z} + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{Z} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{Y} \end{align} and  egin{align}
onumber \dist{X}{Y|Y-Z} - \dist{X}{Y} & \leqslant 	frac{1}{2}  igl(\ent{Y-Z} - \ent{Z} igr) \ & = 	frac{1}{2} \dist{Y}{Z} + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{Y} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{Z}. \end{align} \end{lemma}  egin{proof} We first prove~\eqref{lem51-a}. We may assume (taking an independent copy) that $X$ is independent of $Y, Z$. Then we have  egin{align*} \dist{X}{Y-Z} & - \dist{X}{Y} \ & = \ent{X - Y + Z} - \ent{X-Y} - 	frac{1}{2}\ent{Y - Z} + 	frac{1}{2} \ent{Y}.\end{align*} Combining this with~\eqref{kv-2} (applied with $Y$ replaced by $-Y$) gives the required bound. The second form of the result is immediate from the definition of $\dist{Y}{Z}$, since $Y, Z$ are independent. Turning to~\eqref{ruzsa-3}, we have  egin{align*} \I{Y : Y-Z} & = \ent{Y} + \ent{Y - Z} - \ent{Y, Y - Z} \ & = \ent{Y} + \ent{Y - Z} - \ent{Y, Z} = \ent{Y - Z} - \ent{Z},\end{align*} and so~\eqref{ruzsa-3} is a consequence of~\Cref{cond-dist-fact}. Once again the second form of the result is immediate from the definition of $\dist{Y}{Z}$. \end{proof} We return to our main task of establishing~\eqref{suff-for-first-est}, and hence~\eqref{first-est}. As usual, in the main setting $G=\mathbf{F}_2^n$ we may replace all minus signs with plus signs in the statement of \Cref{first-useful}. Hence by \Cref{first-useful} (and recalling that $k$ is defined to be $\dist{X_1}{X_2}$) we have \[ \dist{X^0_1}{X_1+	ilde X_2} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} k + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_2} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_1},\] \[ \dist{X^0_2}{X_2+	ilde X_1} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} k + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_1} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_2},\]  egin{equation} \dist{X_1^0}{X_1|X_1+	ilde X_2} - \dist{X_1^0}{X_1} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} k + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_1} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_2} \end{equation} and  egin{equation} \dist{X_2^0}{X_2|X_2+	ilde X_1} - \dist{X_2^0}{X_2} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2}k + 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_2} - 	frac{1}{4} \ent{X_1}. \end{equation} Adding all these inequalities, we obtain~\eqref{suff-for-first-est}. For use in the next two sections, we note that subtracting~\eqref{second-tc2} from~\eqref{second-main}, and combining the resulting inequality with~\eqref{second-dist1} and~\eqref{second-dist2} gives the bound \[ \dist{X_1+	ilde X_2}{X_2+	ilde X_1} \leqslant (1 + \eta) k - I_1, \] which is equivalent to  egin{equation}  igH{X_1+X_2+	ilde X_1+	ilde X_2} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} \ent{X_1}+	frac{1}{2} \ent{X_2} + (2 + \eta) k - I_1. \end{equation} One could also bound the left-hand side using~\eqref{kv-2} (twice), but by making use of our hypothesis~\eqref{to-contradict-avg} as above we obtain a slightly better constant.

In this section we conclude the proof of \Cref{de-prop}. Let us begin by recording an inequality which will be used several times in the calculations below.  egin{lemma} Let $X, Y, Z, Z'$ be random variables taking values in some abelian group, and with $Y, Z, Z'$ independent. Then we have  egin{align}
onumber & \dist{X}{Y - Z | Y - Z - Z'} - \dist{X}{Y} \ & \qquad \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} ( \ent{Y - Z - Z'} + \ent{Y - Z} - \ent{Y} - \ent{Z'}). \end{align} \end{lemma}  egin{proof} By~\eqref{ruzsa-3} (with a change of variables) we have \[ \dist{X}{Y - Z | Y - Z - Z'} - \dist{X}{Y - Z} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2}( \ent{Y - Z - Z'} - \ent{Z'}).\] Adding this to~\eqref{lem51-a} gives the result. \end{proof} Turning now to the main argument, let $X_1,X_2,	ilde X_1,	ilde X_2$ be as before, and introduce the random variables \[ U \coloneqq X_1 + X_2, \qquad V \coloneqq 	ilde X_1 + X_2, \qquad W \coloneqq X_1 + 	ilde X_1\] and \[ S \coloneqq X_1 + X_2 + 	ilde X_1 + 	ilde X_2.\] From the definitions~\eqref{first-est-a},~\eqref{second-est-a},~\eqref{third-est-a} of $I_1, I_2, I_3$ and the above notation, we see that \[ I_1 = \I{U : V \, | \, S}, \qquad I_2 = \I{W : U \, | \, S}, \qquad I_3 = \I{V : W \, | \,S}. \] Recall from~\eqref{first-est} that $I_1 \leqslant 2 \eta k$. From~\eqref{second-est} (and since $I_2 = I_3$) we have the inequalities \[ \I{V : W \, | \,S} , \; \I{W : U \, | \, S} \leqslant 2 \eta k +  rac{2\eta(2 \eta k - I_1)}{1-\eta} . \] Summing these two inequalities and the equality $\I{U : V \, | \, S} = I_1$ gives  egin{align} 
onumber \I{U : V \, | \, S} + \I{V : W \, | \,S} &+ \I{W : U \, | \, S}\ 
onumber &\leqslant I_1+4\eta k+  rac{4\eta(2 \eta k - I_1)}{1-\eta}\ &= 6 \eta k -  rac{1 - 5 \eta}{1-\eta} (2 \eta k - I_1). \end{align} We encourage the reader to read the argument assuming first that $I_1 = 2 \eta k$, in which case the calculations are cleaner. We assemble some preliminary estimates on distances. By \Cref{second-useful} (again replacing all minus signs with plus signs, and taking $X = X_1^0$, $Y = X_1$, $Z = X_2$ and $Z' = 	ilde X_1 + 	ilde X_2$, so that $Y + Z = U$ and $Y + Z + Z' = S$) we have, noting that $\ent{Y+Z} = \ent{Z'}$, \[ \dist{X^0_1}{U|S} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} - \ent{X_1}). \] Further applications of \Cref{second-useful} give  egin{align*} \dist{X^0_2}{U|S} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2} &\leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} - \ent{X_2}) \ \dist{X^0_1}{V|S} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} &\leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} - \ent{X_1})\ \dist{X^0_2}{V|S} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2} &\leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} - \ent{X_2}) \end{align*} and \[ \dist{X^0_1}{W|S} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} + \ent{W} - \ent{X_1} - \ent{W'}),\] where $W' := X_2 + 	ilde X_2$. To treat $\dist{X^0_2}{W|S}$, first note that this equals $\dist{X^0_2}{W'|S}$, since for a fixed choice $s$ of $S$ we have $W' = W + s$. Now we may apply \Cref{second-useful} to obtain \[ \dist{X^0_2}{W'|S} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2} \leqslant 	frac{1}{2} (\ent{S} + \ent{W'} - \ent{X_2} - \ent{W}).\] Summing these six estimates and using~\eqref{HS-bound}, we conclude that  egin{align} 
onumber \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{A\in\{U,V,W\}}  ig(\dist{X^0_i}{A|S} & - \dist{X^0_i}{X_i} ig) \ &\leqslant 3\ent{S} - 	frac{3}{2} \ent{X_1} - 	frac{3}{2}\ent{X_2} 
onumber \ &\leqslant (6 - 3\eta) k + 3(2 \eta k - I_1). \end{align} Now we come to the key observation we will exploit, which is that  egin{equation} U + V + W = 0. \end{equation} Here, of course, we are using the fact that we are in characteristic $2$. This is the only critical use of this fact in the argument, in the sense that it cannot be avoided by judicious insertion of negative signs or by accepting slightly worse constants by invoking the inequality $\dist{X}{-Y} \leqslant 3 \dist{X}{Y}$ (see~\cite[Equation 17]{tao-entropy}) and related estimates. To see the force of~\eqref{uvw-s} and~\eqref{uvw-sum}, we state the following general claim.  egin{lemma} Let $G=\mathbf{F}_2^n$ and let $(T_1,T_2,T_3)$ be a $G^3$-valued random variable such that $T_1+T_2+T_3=0$ holds identically. Set  egin{equation} \delta \coloneqq \sum_{1 \leqslant i < j \leqslant 3} \I{T_i;T_j}. \end{equation} Then there exist random variables $T'_1, T'_2$ such that  egin{align*} \dist{T'_1}{T'_2} + & \eta (\dist{X_1^0}{T'_1} - \dist{X_1^0}{X_1}) + \eta(\dist{X_2^0}{T'_2} - \dist{X_2^0}{X_2}) \ & \leqslant \delta +  rac{\eta}{3}  iggl( \delta + \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{j = 1}^3 (\dist{X^0_i}{T_j} - \dist{X^0_i}{X_i})  iggr). \end{align*} \end{lemma} We recommend that the reader first work through this proof in the case $\delta = 0$, when the variables $T_1, T_2, T_3$ are independent. Recalling~\eqref{eq:i-eq-0}, the proof below collapses to taking $T'_1,T'_2$ to be some two of $\{T_1,T_2,T_3\}$, without the need to apply \Cref{lem-bsg}.  egin{proof} We apply the variant of the entropic Balog--Szemer'edi--Gowers theorem stated in \Cref{lem-bsg}, taking $(A,B) = (T_1, T_2)$ there. Since $T_1 + T_2 = T_3$, the conclusion is that  egin{align} 
onumber \sum_{t_3}p_{T_3}(t_3) & \dist{(T_1 | T_3 = t_3)}{(T_2 | T_3 = t_3)} \ & \leqslant 3 \I{T_1 : T_2} + 2 \ent{T_3} - \ent{T_1} - \ent{T_2}. \end{align} The right-hand side in~\eqref{bsg-t1t2} can be rearranged as  egin{align*} & 2( \ent{T_1} + \ent{T_2} + \ent{T_3}) - 3 \ent{T_1,T_2} \ & = 2(\ent{T_1} + \ent{T_2} + \ent{T_3}) - \ent{T_1,T_2} - \ent{T_2,T_3} - \ent{T_1, T_3} = \delta,\end{align*} using the fact that all three terms $\ent{T_i,T_j}$ are equal to $\ent{T_1,T_2,T_3}$ and hence to each other. We also have  egin{align*} & \sum_{t_3}p_{T_3}(t_3)  igl(\dist{X^0_1}{(T_1 | T_3=t_3)} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} igr) \ &\quad = \dist{X^0_1}{T_1 | T_3} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} \leqslant \dist{X^0_1}{T_1} - \dist{X^0_1}{X_1} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{T_1 : T_3} \end{align*} by \Cref{cond-dist-fact}, and similarly  egin{align*} & \sum_{t_3}p_{T_3}(t_3) (\dist{X^0_2}{(T_2 | T_3=t_3)} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2}) \ &\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad \leqslant \dist{X^0_2}{T_2} - \dist{X^0_2}{X_2} + 	frac{1}{2} \I{T_2 : T_3}. \end{align*} Temporarily define \[ \psi[Y_1; Y_2] \coloneqq \dist{Y_1}{Y_2} + \eta (\dist{X_1^0}{Y_1} - \dist{X_1^0}{X_1}) + \eta(\dist{X_2^0}{Y_2} - \dist{X_2^0}{X_2}).\] Putting the above observations together, we have  egin{align*} \sum_{t_3}p_{T_3}(t_3) \psi[(T_1 | T_3=t_3); (T_2 | T_3=t_3)] \leqslant \delta + \eta (\dist{X^0_1}{T_1}-\dist{X^0_1}{X_1}) \ + \eta (\dist{X^0_2}{T_2}-\dist{X^0_2}{X_2}) + 	frac12 \eta \I{T_1:T_3} + 	frac12 \eta \I{T_2:T_3}. \end{align*} Choosing some $t_3$ in the support of $T_3$ that minimizes the $\psi[-;-]$ value, and setting $T'_{1,3} \coloneqq (T_1 | T_3 = t_3)$, $T'_{2,3} \coloneqq (T_2 | T_3 = t_3)$, we have  egin{align}
onumber \psi[T_{1,3}';T_{2,3}'] \leqslant \delta + \eta (& \dist{X^0_1}{T_1}-\dist{X^0_1}{X_1}) + \eta (\dist{X^0_2}{T_2}-\dist{X^0_2}{X_2}) \ & + 	frac12 \eta \I{T_1:T_3} + 	frac12 \eta \I{T_2:T_3}. \end{align} We now repeat this analysis for all permutations of $\{T_1,T_2,T_3\}$ to get variables $T'_{\alpha,\gamma}, T'_{ eta,\gamma}$ for $\{\alpha, eta,\gamma\}$ ranging over all six permutations of $\{1,2,3\}$. Averaging the resulting inequalities~\eqref{eq:t1323}, and recalling the definition~\eqref{delta-t1t2t3-def} of $\delta$, we get \[ 	frac16 \sum_{\alpha, eta,\gamma} \psi[T_{\alpha,\gamma}';T_{ eta,\gamma}'] \leqslant \delta +  rac{\eta}{3}  iggl( \delta + \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{j = 1}^3 (\dist{X^0_i}{T_j}-\dist{X^0_i}{X_i})  iggr), \] from which the result follows (taking $T'_1,T'_2$ to be $T'_{\alpha,\gamma},T'_{ eta,\gamma}$ for some $(\alpha, eta,\gamma)$ that gives at most the average value). \end{proof} Applying \Cref{lem:abstract} with any random variables $(T_1,T_2,T_3)$ such that $T_1+T_2+T_3=0$ holds identically, and applying~\eqref{to-contradict} with $X'_1 = T'_1$, $X'_2 = T'_2$, we deduce that \[ k \leqslant \delta +  rac{\eta}{3}  iggl( \delta + \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{j = 1}^3 (\dist{X^0_i}{T_j} -\dist{X^0_i}{X_i})  iggr). \] Note that $\delta$ is still defined by~\eqref{delta-t1t2t3-def} and thus depends on $T_1,T_2,T_3$. In particular we may apply this for \[ T_1 = (U | S = s),\qquad T_2 = (V | S = s), \qquad T_3 = (W | S = s) \] for $s$ in the range of $S$ (which is a valid choice by~\eqref{uvw-sum}) and then average over $s$ with weights $p_S(s)$, to obtain  egin{equation} k \leqslant 	ilde \delta +  rac{\eta}{3}  iggl( 	ilde \delta + \sum_{i=1}^2 \sum_{A\in\{U,V,W\}}  igl( \dist{X^0_i}{A|S} - \dist{X^0_i}{X_i} igr)  iggr), \end{equation} where \[ 	ilde \delta \coloneqq \I{U : V | S} + \I{V : W | S} + \I{W : U | S}. \] Putting this together with~\eqref{uvw-s} and~\eqref{total-dist}, we conclude that  egin{align*} k &\leqslant \Bigl(1+ rac{\eta}{3}\Bigr)\Bigl(6\eta k- rac{1-5\eta}{1-\eta}(2\eta k-I_1)\Bigr)+ rac{\eta}{3}\Bigl((6-3\eta)k+3(2\eta k-I_1)\Bigr)\ &= (8\eta + \eta^2) k -  iggl(  rac{1 - 5 \eta}{1-\eta}\Bigl(1 +  rac{\eta}{3}\Bigr) - \eta  iggr)(2 \eta k - I_1)\ &\leqslant (8 \eta + \eta^2) k \end{align*} since the quantity $2 \eta k - I_1$ is non-negative (by~\eqref{first-est}), and its coefficient in the above expression is non-positive provided that $\eta(2\eta + 17) \leqslant 3$, which is certainly the case for our choice $\eta =  rac{1}{9}$ (and in fact for any $\eta \leqslant  rac{1}{6}$). Moreover, for ootnote{In fact we can take any $\eta< rac{1}{4 + \sqrt{17}} =  rac{1}{8.1231\dots}$, and the other constants in the paper can be improved accordingly. In \cite{liao1,liao2} the arguments were modified to also apply in the regime $\eta <  rac{1}{8}$.} $\eta=	frac{1}{9}$ we have $8 \eta + \eta^2 < 1$. It follows that $k=0$, as desired. The proof of \Cref{de-prop} (and hence of all our results) is complete.
